  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ intersects the line $2x-y=-3.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation $2x-y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+3-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+4{x}^{2}+4x+1 & =4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+12x+13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={12}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =144-260 \\\\  & =-116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-116<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}+12x+13=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ does not intersect the line $2x-y=-3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/00bc159e9c25b913454c6037a822e41931c6224f3e6f0180da550fe9c857e718.png)",
